light-bug Diagnoses and fixes a Delphi crash report (madExcept .mad) end-to-end through a successful build, for any product registered in products.ini (BioniX, DNA Baser, ...). Lists the recent .mad attachments across the product's Thunderbird mbox(es), you pick one, then the light-bug agent reads the call stack, checks the Fixes Log for duplicates, and either reports "already fixed" or fixes + compiles. Never releases — you review the diff and release manually. \nUse this skill when: you say "/light-bug", "/light-bug bionix", "new bug report", "check the latest crash".
light-code-ArchitectureClass Audits a Delphi project's own classes — INCLUDING forms — for ill-shaped classes: god classes / low cohesion, anemic data classes, misplaced responsibility (feature envy), fat config records, shared mutable singletons, type-erased back-references, and Ex-subclass-reached-by-downcast. Reports ranked reshaping recommendations (extract-class, move-method, replace-singleton, ...) and always saves a full HandOver.md; never performs the refactor. \nUse this skill when: you say "is this a god class", "review the class design", "what class should be split", "find anemic classes". For unit (file) merges use light-code-ArchitectureUnit.
light-code-ArchitectureUnit Audits a Delphi project's own units (files) for SHALLOW modules and the coupling problems that mean two or more units should become one deep module — information leakage, temporal decomposition, pass-through layers, always-paired usage (Ousterhout's deep-module model). Reports ranked, evidence-backed merge candidates with a deep-module design for each; never performs the merge. Skips LightSaber and forms by default. \nUse this skill when: you say "find shallow units", "what units should be merged", "is this over-split", especially after several feature additions. For class-level shapes use light-code-ArchitectureClass.
light-code-CheckOsCompatibility Audits Delphi FMX source for cross-platform compatibility issues — Windows-only units/APIs, hardcoded paths, missing platform conditionals ({$IFDEF}), and mobile-incompatible patterns. Reports only; it does not apply fixes. Launches the light-code-CheckOsCompatibility agent. \nUse this skill when: you say "check this for Android/iOS", "is this safe on mobile/macOS/Linux", "audit Lib/ for cross-platform issues", or before targeting a new OS.
light-code-StyleChecker Scans imported / 3rd-party Delphi code for style compliance, common mistakes, and dangerous patterns (resource leaks, unsafe casts, missing try/finally). Launches the light-code-StyleChecker agent, which also applies mandatory reformatting passes (LightSaber unit header, keyword casing, if/then/else layout, no line wrapping). NOT for our own project code. \nUse this skill when: you say "check this 3rd-party unit", "scan the imported library", "style-check SourceCode/". For our own code use /light-review-Full.
light-md-DelphiIdiom Fixes Delphi vocabulary and writing-clarity issues in Markdown docs — swaps non-Delphi terms for Delphi ones (null to nil, enum to enumeration, lambda to anonymous method, module to unit, ...) and applies bounded clarity rewrites at a high confidence bar. Launches the light-md-DelphiIdiom agent (MD only by default; PAS comments only on explicit request). Skips book/manuscript prose. \nUse this skill when: you say "check vocab", "fix vocabulary", "delphi terms", "clean up the md", or after writing/editing *.md in a Delphi project.
light-md-DriftUpdate Scans project markdown docs (CLAUDE.md, README, docs/*.md) for drift after code changes — flags sections out of sync with the current code (class names, file paths, settings keys, procedure signatures, architecture claims) and proposes concrete edits, editing only where a doc claim genuinely contradicts the code. \nUse this skill when: you say "update md", "check docs", "refresh claude.md", "verify docs", or after a change that may have outdated the docs.
light-md-PruneClaudeMD Prunes and tightens a CLAUDE.md (or any agent/skill instruction markdown) — cuts bloat and duplication, deletes stale/wrong/contradictory rules, relocates misplaced content to the right layer, and reorders for adherence, without losing load-bearing info. Backs the file up first; verifies before cutting; flags uncertain items instead of deleting. Hand-typed only. \nUse this skill when: you say "clean up this CLAUDE.md", "my CLAUDE.md is too long", "prune the docs", or "Claude keeps ignoring my rules".
light-new-Align One-question-at-a-time design interview before any non-trivial Delphi task (a feature, a refactor, a bug investigation, a design decision). Each question carries the AI's own recommended answer; it skips everything it can decide itself (an auto-decided list) and asks the highest-stakes Delphi questions first — binary-compatibility, the LightSaber boundary, persistence. Reaches a shared design concept BEFORE any plan or code. Adaptive: ~5 questions for a tiny task, up to ~40 for a major one. \nUse this skill when: starting any non-trivial feature/refactor/bug, or you say "align first", "interview me", "ask me questions before you build".
light-new-Feature Turns "I want feature X" into a designed, sliced, test-driven Delphi implementation — the front-half counterpart to light-review-Full. Aligns on the design (Phase 1 is the light-new-Align interview), writes a PRD, splits the work into vertical slices, implements each slice red-green (failing DUnitX test first) compiling via the light-compiler agent, then hands off to light-review-PostEdit / light-review-Full. Also has a brand-new-app path (copies a LightSaber template). \nUse this skill when: you say "add a feature", "build feature X", "implement a new feature", "create a new screen/dialog/option", or start a brand-new Delphi app.
light-ref-DesignPatterns Reference: a quick index of the GoF design patterns that actually pay off in Delphi desktop/mobile code, each with its Delphi-native shortcut (anonymous methods, virtual methods, System.Messaging, class functions, enumerators) so you reach for the full pattern only when it earns its keep. \nUse this skill when: choosing a structure for varying behaviour, decoupling producer from consumer, building objects step by step, or wrapping/adapting a subsystem.
light-ref-Memory Reference for Delphi memory-safety and exception handling — the try..finally gold standard, freeing owned fields, FreeAndNil, Owner-managed components, functions that return objects, specific try..except, domain exception hierarchies, bare raise (no garbage collector). Also the single source of truth for the review pipeline's memory checklist. \nUse this skill when: writing or reviewing code that creates objects, manages lifetimes, or handles exceptions.
light-ref-Refactoring Reference catalog of behaviour-preserving Delphi refactorings — Extract Method/Class, Guard Clauses, Named Constants, Replace Conditional with Polymorphism, Introduce Parameter Object (record), Remove with, Rename, Inline Method, and introducing a test seam via a shared base class. Favours composition and concrete typing over interfaces. \nUse this skill when: cleaning up smelly code, taming a long method or god class, or planning a safe refactor.
light-ref-Threading Reference for writing or reviewing Delphi threading on VCL and FMX (incl. Android/iOS) — TThread, TThread.CreateAnonymousThread, the PPL (TTask/TParallel/TFuture), Synchronize vs Queue, TInterlocked/TCriticalSection/TMonitor/TLightweightMREW, thread-safe collections, graceful cancellation. Dense, RTL-source-cited material. \nUse this skill when: writing or reviewing code that works with threads or touches shared state from more than one thread.
light-review-Critical Counter-analyzes the review findings already in the conversation BEFORE fixing anything: verifies every surviving claim against the actual code and docs, drops false positives (one-line reason each), then fixes ALL the issues that remain — not just the easy ones. General-purpose, not only for Delphi. Hands the applied fixes to light-review-PostEdit for the final verify. \nUse this skill when: a review/audit/listing has produced findings and you say "counter-analyze these findings", "vet and fix", "verify then fix all of these".
light-review-DUnitX Reference (knowledge injected into the working context). Structuring Delphi DUnitX unit tests — project layout, [TestFixture]/[Setup]/[TearDown]/[Test], Method_Scenario_Expected naming, the assertion cheat-sheet, substituting a collaborator with a concrete fake (shared base, no interface), and SQLite :memory: integration tests. Pairs with /light-review-RedGreen and /light-review-FakeTest. \nUse this skill when: writing, organising, or reviewing DUnitX tests.
light-review-FakeTest Audits an existing Delphi DUnitX or classic-DUnit test suite for FAKE or WEAK tests — ones that pass without verifying the behavior they name (zero assertions, Assert.Pass-only, tautologies, setup-only checks, tautological helpers, vague error checks, negative tests with no probe). Establishes the green baseline, launches the auditor agent (sharded if large), and can PROVE fakeness with a git-safe mutation pass. Reports REAL / WEAK / SUSPECT per test. Read-only by default. \nUse this skill when: you say "are these tests fake", "make sure the tests aren't fake", "did we game the tests to pass", "audit test integrity".
light-review-Full The full three-stage Delphi code review pipeline for our OWN project code. Stage 1 finds correctness bugs, stage 2 counter-analyzes the findings and applies the fixes that survive, stage 3 verifies those fixes and compiles; then it syncs project docs (light-md-DriftUpdate) and records a dated review-log entry. Orchestrates the light-review-step1/2/3 agents. Not for 3rd-party imports — use light-code-StyleChecker for those. \nUse this skill when: you say "review this Delphi code", "code review FormFoo.pas", "deep review the Lib folder", or want a thorough correctness review of project .pas files.
light-review-PostEdit Verifies the code you just wrote (a bug fix or a new feature) actually works. For each change it confirms the new code matches the stated intent, broke nothing observable, missed no call sites, and didn't break DFM/FMX bindings; reverts anything that doesn't hold up; then runs the project's tests (large change) or compiles (small change). The terminal verification step after any edit. \nUse this skill when: you just implemented a fix or feature and want it checked before trusting it, or you say "/light-review-PostEdit", "verify what you changed".
light-review-RedGreen Red-green TDD for any Delphi change (a new behavior OR a bug fix). Forces the FAILING DUnitX test to be written and confirmed RED first (failing on its assertion, not a compile error), then drives the implementation to green, compiling ONLY through the light-compiler agent. For a bug the failing test is the reproduction — green means fixed and regression-proofed. A shared technique used by both light-new-Feature (Phase 4) and bug fixing. \nUse this skill when: you say "TDD this", "write the test first", "reproduce the bug with a test", "red-green", or before implementing any change with testable logic.
light-security-ClaudeSettingsAudit Audits Claude Code config files (settings.json, settings.local.json, .mcp.json) across the computer for unsafe or malicious content — shell-running hooks, credential/proxy env vars, arbitrary MCP servers, permission-bypass modes, over-broad allow rules. Classifies each finding DANGEROUS / SUSPICIOUS / SAFE against a known-good baseline. Read-only. Hand-typed only. \nUse this skill when: you say "scan my Claude config", "check my settings.local.json for malware", "are my Claude permission files safe".
light-web-CodeReview Reviews HTML, CSS, or JavaScript for errors, formatting, best practices, accessibility, and cross-browser issues. Launches the light-web-CodeReview agent over recently-changed web files by default. \nUse this skill when: you say "review this HTML/CSS/JS", "check my stylesheet", "does this JavaScript look right", or after writing/editing web frontend code.
